- **Adversarial review telemetry:** after each `adversarial-review` workflow run, append that run's per-model telemetry table to `~/.claude/adversarial-review-telemetry.md` under a dated heading, so model performance accumulates across sessions and repos. Read the existing file first (if any) and append; never overwrite. Three rules for the table:
  - **Label rows by the actual backend** (`firerouter` / `glm-latest` / `deepseek-flash-latest` / `kimi-latest`), not the Claude Code canonical slot name (`opus` / `sonnet` / `haiku` / `fable`). The backend that ran is in each run's returned per-agent `model` field — use that, not the slot name.
  - **Maintain a cumulative roll-up at the top of the file** (above the dated sections): total runs, per backend a running `valid` and `unique` count summed across all runs, and a `roundMs` column = the wall-time of that run's full `Workflow()` invocation, measured by the hub (you) around the call — not by the script (the Workflow runtime forbids `Date.now()` inside the script, so per-agent latency can't be tracked from inside). If all agents resolved to one backend in a run, that run contributes to that backend's counts only — the roll-up reflects the actual backends, not the intended panel.
  - **Latency is hub-measured, round-level only.** The `roundMs` in the roll-up is the wall-time of the entire round (Stage 1 + Stage 2), stamped by the hub around the `Workflow()` call. Per-agent or per-lens latency is not available (no clock inside the script). For the matrix whittle, this means you can compare backends on valid/unique *and* on round cost, but not on per-cell latency — a model that's slow per-round and surfaces few unique findings is a cut candidate; one that's slow per-round but surfaces unique things is a keeper.
  - This is the one exception to "never create files" — the user explicitly asked for this log.
